{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q023_homeodomain_heterogeneous_fold","answer":"Homology is still justified, but only at the family level, and only for the classified homeodomain region. In SCOPe 2.08 both d1p7jd_ (sunid 94286, 1p7j chain D) and d1ztra1 (sunid 125655, 1ztr A:0-59) are in family a.4.1.1. They share the same parent species-level node lineage: both sit under sunid 46692, the Drosophila melanogaster species node, within a.4.1.1. Both PDB entries are engrailed homeodomain variants. 1p7j is the crystal structure of the K52E mutant. 1ztr is the NMR structure of the L16A mutant, whose title mentions a denatured state and folding intermediate. Being in one family means a common evolutionary origin for the homeodomain, which is the all-alpha, DNA-binding helix-turn-helix (class a, fold a.4, superfamily a.4.1). The 'hf' label, which I treat as the prompt's heterogeneity flag, does not change that family assignment or undo the homology. The SCOPe API records I retrieved did not expose an hf field, so I cannot confirm its exact definition from the API. I read it as a caution that the entry is not a clean, uniform single-chain domain example.

What the shared family does not justify is treating the two entries as interchangeable templates or training examples. (1) The entries differ experimentally: 1p7j is a crystal structure of a K52E mutant, and d1p7jd_ covers the whole chain D ('1p7j D:'). 1ztr is a solution NMR model of an L16A mutant. L16A is a destabilizing mutation in the hydrophobic core, and the entry was studied for folding and denatured-state behaviour, so its conformational ensemble and helix packing may differ from the native fold. (2) The domain boundaries differ. d1ztra1 is residues A:0-59, and the chain also carries a separate artifact segment, d1ztra2 (A:-1 to -1), classified as l.1.1.1 in the artifacts class. The 1ztr chain is therefore not one homeodomain-only record, and the hf-annotated domain should not be assumed equivalent to a whole-chain domain such as d1p7jd_. (3) Point mutations, termini, tags and experimental method change the coordinates, completeness and quality (the API also provides per-entry quality data, which I did not use). Residue-level mapping, loop and terminal conformation, and DNA-contact geometry can differ even though the fold is shared. (4) A family label does not carry over function or binding specificity. A homeodomain in the 1p7j crystal entry may bind DNA with specific contacts, whereas the 1ztr denatured-state study says nothing about DNA recognition. Family membership also does not mean the same sequence identity, ligand state or oligomer state.

For practice: use the family label for homology and fold-level grouping. When building templates, benchmarks or train/test splits, choose representatives by experimental method, mutation, completeness, resolution or NMR ensemble quality, and domain boundary. Do not use the two entries as duplicates or as stand-ins for each other. Keep them in the same cluster or split, because they are homologous and nearly identical in sequence, to avoid leakage. Treat d1ztra1 as a flagged, less-clean example, and check the hf meaning and the full 1ztr entry before using it as a canonical template.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases","/api/v1/releases/2.08","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1p7jd_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d1ztra1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/a.4.1.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/94286","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/125655","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/46692","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1p7j/domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ztr/heterogens","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1p7jd_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ztra2","pretrained model knowledge of SCOPe hierarchy"]}]}
